From ef8bdea70093d3dc8ab0a510c942c0eabf2b365b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:26:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] target/m68k: set the M bit on the first write to a page
 loaded by a read

On the 68040, when an ATC entry for a writable page was created by a
read access and the page descriptor M bit is clear, the first write to
that page triggers a table search that sets M.  QEMU created a writable
TLB entry on read, so a later write never walked the tables again and M
stayed clear.  Guests relying on M to track dirty pages (e.g. the
NetBSD m68k pmap) could then miss modifications.

Do not grant PAGE_WRITE when filling the TLB for a non-store access to
a page whose M bit is clear.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
---
 target/m68k/helper.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/target/m68k/helper.c b/target/m68k/helper.c
index 5f91d206f5..cdba8373df 100644
--- a/target/m68k/helper.c
+++ b/target/m68k/helper.c
@@ -890,6 +890,14 @@ static int get_physical_address(CPUM68KState *env, hwaddr *physical,
         if (access_type & ACCESS_STORE) {
             return -1;
         }
+    } else if (!(access_type & ACCESS_STORE) &&
+               !(next & M68K_DESC_MODIFIED)) {
+        /*
+         * The page has not been modified yet: do not let a read fill a
+         * writable TLB entry, so that the first write walks the tables
+         * again and sets the M bit, as the 68040 ATC does.
+         */
+        *prot &= ~PAGE_WRITE;
     }
     if (next & M68K_DESC_SUPERONLY) {
         if ((access_type & ACCESS_SUPER) == 0) {
-- 
